    <h1>Thick-billed Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Thick-billed Raven (<em>Corvus crassirostris</em>) is a broad omnivore. Carrion is a major dietary component — the species' immense bill is specifically adapted for opening carcasses and bones. The diet also includes invertebrates, grain, fruit, small vertebrates, eggs, and abundant human food waste in and around Ethiopian cities and villages.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on field observations and species accounts in the <em>Handbook of the Birds of the World</em> (HBW) and published Ethiopian ornithological surveys:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> dead livestock (cattle, sheep, goats), roadkill, and large wildlife carcasses. The Thick-billed Raven is often the dominant bird at carcasses in its range, displacing smaller scavengers.</li>
      <li><strong>Bones and marrow:</strong> the powerful, arched bill can crack bones that are inaccessible to other corvids. Bone-prying behaviour has been observed; the species may carry bones to rocks and drop them (osteophagy) analogous to Lammergeier behaviour, though this is less systematically documented.</li>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, earthworms — ground-foraging for invertebrates is common, particularly after rain.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> cereal grain (teff, wheat, barley) from agricultural fields — the species raids crops and forages in recently ploughed fields.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> various fruits and berries taken opportunistically; figs recorded.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> lizards, mice, nestlings and eggs of ground-nesting birds — taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> a major resource in the Ethiopian Highlands. The species is abundant around Addis Ababa, Gondar, Lalibela, and other highland towns, exploiting rubbish tips, abattoirs, market waste, and food scraps.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Bill as a Foraging Tool</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Thick-billed Raven's bill is the largest and most massively constructed of any corvid — length 7–8 cm, strongly arched, laterally compressed, and tipped with white. This bill shape provides:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>High bite force</strong> — for cracking bones, crushing hard invertebrates, and prying at carcasses.</li>
      <li><strong>Prying leverage</strong> — for opening carcasses at joints and forcing access to soft tissue inside a carcass that smaller birds cannot reach.</li>
      <li><strong>Manipulation precision</strong> — the bill tip, though robust, can still handle small food items.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This combination of power and dexterity makes the Thick-billed Raven an ecological generalist with access to food items unavailable to sympatric corvids such as the Fan-tailed Raven (<em>Corvus rhipidurus</em>) and the Pied Crow (<em>Corvus albus</em>).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Urban and Agricultural Foraging</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Thick-billed Raven is a highly synanthropic species (closely associated with human settlements). In the Ethiopian Highlands, it is one of the most visible large birds in highland towns. Rubbish tips and abattoirs around Addis Ababa support large resident populations. Agricultural land — particularly teff and barley fields — is exploited for grain, especially after ploughing and during harvest. The species is considered an agricultural pest in some areas.</p>
  </section>

